# M7 — queryAffectingOne's row-count assertion disabled.
#
# V7: a failing RLS USING clause affects ZERO rows and raises NOTHING, reproduced three
# ways. V19: a cross-user DELETE returns `DELETE 0`, byte-identical to deleting a
# nonexistent id. `queryAffectingOne` is the code that turns that silence into a
# RowCountError, so with the assertion disabled a DENIED write returns success to its
# caller.
#
# It stayed green at 44/44 because the suite's cross-user write assertions went through
# psql and read the row count themselves, never through queryAffectingOne.
#
# Both directions are named, and deliberately: `if (false)` admits zero rows AND admits
# five. A control that only tested the zero case would pass against `rowCount < 1`.
id: M7
title: queryAffectingOne's `rowCount !== 1` assertion replaced by `if (false)`
expect: caught
proves: the V7 silence-is-not-success assertion rejects BOTH too few and too many affected rows
expect-test: raises RowCountError when the update affects ZERO rows
expect-test: raises RowCountError when the update affects MORE than one row

file: tests/support/scoped-pg/scoped.ts
count: 1
@@FIND
    if (result.rowCount !== 1) {
@@REPLACE
    if (false) {
@@END
